<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lubawa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lubawa, in German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobau">Löbau</a>, is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in present-day Warmian-Masurian Voivodeship. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s conquest it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Culm</a>, who had a castle there and ruled the surrounding district as their own territory. With the rest of the Culm diocese it passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> in 1466.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lubawa.jpg" alt="Lubawa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zbigniew.czernik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lubawa_-_fontanna_na_Rynku_-_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
